SUMMARY:Applied Micro – Brian Knight (Brown University) – The Partisan 
 Consequences of Racial Gerrymandering: New Evidence from the 1990s
LOCATION:Room 404\, FBE Building\, 111 Barry St\, Carlton VIC 3053
DESCRIPTION:Brian Knight: “The Partisan Consequences of Racial Gerrymande
 ring: New Evidence from the 1990s”\nhttps://sites.brown.edu/brianknight/
 \n\nZoom: https://unimelb.zoom.us/j/84741930353?pwd=1TDakj4y62yHU6dWbk6ZMF
 ehNtmGMq.1&from=addon\n\nGoogle Maps: https://maps.app.goo.gl/BirLkJKurX9p
 SbBB8\n\nABSTRACT:\nWe examine the partisan consequences of racial gerryma
 ndering\, which creates majority-minority districts. In our theoretical mo
 del\, racial gerrymandering benefits the non-dominant party. That is\, rac
 ial gerrymandering reduces expected Democratic seats in blue states but in
 creases expected Democratic seats in red states. The empirical analysis de
 velops counterfactual election results using a partisan prediction model. 
 This model links district-level presidential returns under alternative map
 s and congressional vote shares\, thereby holding voter preferences fixed.
  Applying this framework to redistricting in 1991 shows that racial gerrym
 andering increased safe Democratic seats while turning adjacent districts 
 right-leaning\, yielding net seat gains for Republicans. These findings cl
 arify historical realignments in partisan support over time\, with Republi
 cans supporting racial gerrymandering in 1991 but opposing it more recentl
 y.
URL:https://unimelb.zoom.us/j/84741930353?pwd=1TDakj4y62yHU6dWbk6ZMFehNtmGM
 q.1&from=addon
END:VEVENT
