SUMMARY:Econometrics – Mitchell O’Hara-Wild (Monash) – Graphs for for
 ecasting many related time series
LOCATION:Room 404\, FBE Building\, 111 Barry St\, Carlton VIC 3053
DESCRIPTION:Mitchell O’Hara-Wild: “Graphs for forecasting many related 
 time series”\nhttps://mitchelloharawild.com/\n\nZoom: https://unimelb.zo
 om.us/j/85966225829?pwd=g6hOdHyvYB0qta0aFzGbWOgczJ1Qzr.1\n\nGoogle Maps: h
 ttps://maps.app.goo.gl/BirLkJKurX9pSbBB8\n\nABSTRACT:\nForecasting in prac
 tice rarely involves a single time series. Organisations forecast large co
 llections of related series\, such as product sales across stores and regi
 ons\, which requires software that handles many series and models consiste
 ntly and produces probabilistic forecasts that can be evaluated\, combined
 \, and compared. The fable R package addresses this by treating forecastin
 g as a workflow of composable stages designed with tidy data analysis prin
 ciples. The relationships between related time series are commonly encoded
  with aggregation constraint matrices\, which forecast reconciliation uses
  to produce coherent and more accurate forecasts. Directed acyclic graphs 
 (DAGs) offer a more general representation that make large collections of 
 series easier to explore and manipulate. As series are disaggregated by ma
 ny attributes\, their number grows rapidly\, increasing computational cost
  and degrading accuracy through noisy\, uninformative\, or misspecified se
 ries. Using time series features and graph search algorithms\, uninformati
 ve subgraphs can be efficiently pruned while preserving coherency\, substa
 ntially reducing the number of series to forecast while retaining most of 
 the information. This seminar begins with the software design of fable for
  forecasting at scale\, then introduces graph-based forecast reconciliatio
 n and feature-based graph pruning for accurate forecasting at scale.
URL:https://unimelb.zoom.us/j/85966225829?pwd=g6hOdHyvYB0qta0aFzGbWOgczJ1Qz
 r.1
END:VEVENT
