SUMMARY:PhD Brown Bag Seminar – Sonia Ma (University of Melbourne) – Me
 rchants Away\, Widows at Home: Mercantile Activity and Female Chastity in 
 Late Imperial China
LOCATION:Room 304\, FBE Building\, 111 Barry St\, Carlton VIC 3062
DESCRIPTION:Sonia Ma: “Merchants Away\, Widows at Home: Mercantile Activi
 ty and Female Chastity in Late Imperial China”\n\nGoogle Maps: https://m
 aps.app.goo.gl/BirLkJKurX9pSbBB8\n\nABSTRACT:\nDoes commercial expansion i
 nevitably erode traditional social norms\, or can it reinforce them? This 
 paper studies how male commercial sojourning shaped female chastity in lat
 e imperial China. I use the native-place origins of guilds\, associations 
 formed by merchants conducting business away from home\, to measure outwar
 d merchant activity\, and link them to county-level gazetteer records of c
 haste women. Counties with stronger traditions of outward merchant activit
 y recorded more chaste women\, and the relationship remains positive withi
 n counties over time. I propose lineage institutions as a mechanism: prolo
 nged male absence may have increased reliance on patrilineal kin to suppor
 t households and regulate female conduct. Consistent with this mechanism\,
  merchant-sending counties also show a higher density of Qing-period clan 
 genealogies. The results suggest that commercial expansion could reinforce
  rather than weaken traditional gender institutions: as trade made men mor
 e mobile\, the institutions governing women who remained at home may have 
 become stronger. However\, the evidence remains correlational\, and the ce
 ntral challenge is identifying exogenous variation in outward merchant act
 ivity.
END:VEVENT
